mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(220,394) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"31\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-31\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" transform=\"translate(220,-345) scale(0.707)\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mn\"><use data-c=\"39\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-39\"></use></g></g><rect width=\"553.6\" height=\"60\" x=\"120\" y=\"220\"></rect></g></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container>"},{"identifier":"D","content":"81"}],"correct_options":["B"],"explanation":"<p>To determine how the stress in the leg changes when a man's linear dimensions increase by a factor of 9, we can analyze the relationship between the dimensions and the stress on the legs.</p>\n\n<p>First, let's establish some relationships:\n\n<ol>\n\n<li>Linear dimensions (length, width, height) increase by a factor of 9.</li><br>\n\n<li>Density remains the same.</li>\n\n</ol>\n\n<p>Stress is defined as force per unit area:</p>\n\n<p><mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -2.059ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"18.945ex\" height=\"5.285ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -1426 8373.6 2336\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mtext\" style=\"font-family: Roboto,'Tiro Devanagari Hindi','Tiro Bangla',sans-serif;\"><text data-variant=\"-explicitFont\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\" font-size=\"884px\">Stress</text></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(3877.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"3D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-3D\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mstyle\" transform=\"translate(4933.6,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mfrac\"><g data-mml-node=\"mtext\" style=\"font-family: Roboto,'Tiro Devanagari Hindi','Tiro Bangla',sans-serif;\" transform=\"translate(220,676)\"><text data-variant=\"-explicitFont\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\" font-size=\"884px\">Force</text></g><g data-mml-node=\"mtext\" style=\"font-family: Roboto,'Tiro Devanagari Hindi','Tiro Bangla',sans-serif;\" transform=\"translate(520,-710)\"><text data-variant=\"-explicitFont\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\" font-size=\"884px\">Area</text></g><rect width=\"3200\" height=\"60\" x=\"120\" y=\"220\"></rect></g></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container></p>\n\n<p>Since density remains constant, the volume and thus the mass of the man will change according to the cube of the linear dimensions. Since the linear dimension changes by a factor of 9, the volume (and hence the mass) changes by a factor of:</p>\n\n<p><mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.05ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"8.53ex\" height=\"1.935ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -833.2 3770.1 855.2\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"msup\"><g data-mml-node=\"mn\"><use data-c=\"39\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-39\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(533,363) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"33\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-33\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(1214.3,0)\"><use data-c=\"3D\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-3D\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(2270.1,0)\"><use data-c=\"37\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-37\"></use><use data-c=\"32\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-32\" transform=\"translate(500,0)\"></use><use data-c=\"39\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-39\" transform=\"translate(1000,0)\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container></p>\n\n<p>Therefore, the weight (force due to gravity) also increases by a factor of 729.</p>\n\n<p>The cross-sectional area of the leg is proportional to the square of the linear dimensions. Thus, if the linear dimension increases by a factor of 9, the cross-sectional area increases by a factor of:</p>\n\n<p><mjx-conta